{
  "format": "srwb",
  "format_version": "1.0",
  "exported_at": "2026-03-12T00:00:00.000Z",
  "notebook": {
    "id": "nb-prolog-gen-r-001",
    "name": "Prolog Generates R: Compiler Demo",
    "description": "Demonstrates UnifyWeaver compiling Prolog predicates into R code. Part 1: factorial (linear→tail transformation). Part 2: ancestor (transitive closure vs skip/pattern).",
    "kernelLanguage": null,
    "cellCounter": 26,
    "cells": [
      {
        "code": "# Prolog Generates R — UnifyWeaver Compiler Demo\n\nThis workbook demonstrates **UnifyWeaver** compiling Prolog logic into executable R code — live, inside a notebook.\n\n## Part 1: Factorial (Linear Recursion)\n1. Load the UnifyWeaver compiler environment\n2. Define a recursive `factorial/2` in Prolog\n3. The compiler detects the recursion pattern and generates an equivalent R loop\n4. The generated R code is written to another cell via `nb_write/3`\n5. Run the R cell to verify correctness\n\n## Part 2: Ancestor (Transitive Closure & Pattern Control)\n8. Define `parent/2` facts and `ancestor/2` in Prolog\n9. Compile with default pattern → transitive closure (BFS graph traversal)\n10. Compile again with `skip(transitive_closure)` → different generated code\n11. Compare the two approaches side by side",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 1: Load UnifyWeaver\n\nLoad the UnifyWeaver environment and the R target compiler.\n\n**Note:** In swipl-wasm, use `use_module(unifyweaver(...))` (search path alias) instead of `use_module(library(...))`. The `['../init'].` call sets up the `unifyweaver` search path so modules resolve correctly.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "['../init'].\n:- use_module(unifyweaver(targets/r_target)).\n:- use_module(unifyweaver(core/advanced/linear_recursion)).\n:- use_module(unifyweaver(core/recursive_compiler)).",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "load_uw",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 2: Define Factorial in Prolog\n\nA simple recursive factorial. The base case returns 1 when N is 0. The recursive case computes `N * factorial(N-1)`.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": ":- dynamic factorial/2.\n\nfactorial(0, 1).\nfactorial(N, F) :-\n    N > 0,\n    N1 is N - 1,\n    factorial(N1, F1),\n    F is N * F1.",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "prolog_factorial",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 3: Compile Prolog → R\n\nThe compiler:\n1. Reads the Prolog source from the **prolog_factorial** cell via `nb_read/3`\n2. Consults it to assert the clauses into the database\n3. Calls `compile_linear_recursion/3` which detects the recursion pattern, extracts the step (`N1 is N - 1` → step=1, down), and generates an R `for` loop\n4. Writes the generated R code to the **r_factorial** cell via `nb_write/3`\n\nThe `memo(false)` option requests loop-based compilation instead of memoized reduce.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "%% Read the Prolog source from the previous cell\nuser:nb_read('prolog_factorial', '.code', _PrologSrc),\nformat('Read from prolog_factorial:~n~w~n~n', [_PrologSrc]),\n\n%% Consult it to assert the clauses\nsetup_call_cleanup(\n    open('/tmp/_nb_src.pl', write, _Out),\n    write(_Out, _PrologSrc),\n    close(_Out)),\nconsult('/tmp/_nb_src.pl'),\n\n%% Compile factorial/2 → R\nr_target:init_r_target,\nlinear_recursion:compile_linear_recursion(factorial/2, [target(r), memo(false)], _Code),\nformat('--- Generated R code ---~n~w~n', [_Code]),\n\n%% Write compiler output to the R cell\nuser:nb_write('r_factorial', '.code', _Code),\nwriteln('\\nR code written to cell: r_factorial').",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "compile_to_r",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 4: Run the Generated R Code\n\nThe cell below will contain the compiler-generated `factorial` function. Run it to define the function, then run Step 5 to test it.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "# This cell will be overwritten by the compiler.\n# Run the 'compile_to_r' cell above first.",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "r",
        "name": "r_factorial",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 5: Test the Generated R Code\n\nCall the compiler-generated `factorial` function to verify it produces correct results.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "cat(\"6! =\", factorial(6), \"\\n\")\ncat(\"20! =\", factorial(20), \"\\n\")\ncat(\"0! =\", factorial(0), \"\\n\")",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "r",
        "name": "test_r",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 6: Inspect via Bash\n\nThe Notebook VFS lets any kernel inspect cells. Here Bash reads the compiler-generated code:",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "echo \"=== Cells ===\"\nls /nb/\necho \"\"\necho \"=== r_factorial code ===\"\ncat /nb/r_factorial/.code\necho \"\"\necho \"=== r_factorial language ===\"\ncat /nb/r_factorial/.language",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "bash",
        "name": "inspect",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "# Step 7: Relative addressing — /nb/-1/.output reads the previous cell's output.\n# /nb/./.code reads this cell's own code. Copy-paste to accumulate!\necho \"[accumulate] previous cell output via /nb/-1/.output:\"\necho \"---\"\ncat /nb/-1/.output\necho \"---\"\necho \"\"\necho \"[self] this cell's code via /nb/./.code:\"\ncat /nb/./.code",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "bash",
        "name": "accumulate",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "---\n\n# Part 2: Ancestor — Transitive Closure & Pattern Control\n\nThe same compiler handles relational predicates too. Here we define `ancestor/2` as a transitive closure over `parent/2` facts.\n\nThe compiler recognizes the pattern and generates **BFS graph traversal** code in R. But we can also `skip(transitive_closure)` to force a different compilation strategy — demonstrating how `skip` and `pattern` options control which code the compiler emits.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 8: Define Family Tree in Prolog\n\nA small family tree using `parent/2` facts, and `ancestor/2` defined as a transitive closure.\n\nNote how concise the Prolog is — just 2 clauses for `ancestor/2`. The compiler will generate all the graph traversal machinery.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": ":- dynamic parent/2, ancestor/2.\n\n%% Family tree\nparent(alice, bob).\nparent(bob, charlie).\nparent(bob, diana).\nparent(charlie, eve).\n\n%% Transitive closure: ancestor(X, Y) if X is an ancestor of Y\nancestor(X, Y) :- parent(X, Y).\nancestor(X, Y) :- parent(X, Z), ancestor(Z, Y).",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "prolog_ancestor",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 9: Compile ancestor/2 → R (default: transitive closure)\n\nThe compiler classifies `ancestor/2` as a **transitive closure** over `parent/2` and generates R code with:\n- An adjacency list (environment hash map)\n- BFS traversal functions (`ancestor_all`, `ancestor_check`)\n- An `add_parent` function to populate the graph\n\nThis is the optimal compilation for graph reachability queries.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "%% Consult the ancestor definitions\nuser:nb_read('prolog_ancestor', '.code', _AncSrc),\nformat('Read from prolog_ancestor:~n~w~n~n', [_AncSrc]),\nsetup_call_cleanup(\n    open('/tmp/_nb_anc.pl', write, _Out),\n    write(_Out, _AncSrc),\n    close(_Out)),\nconsult('/tmp/_nb_anc.pl'),\n\n%% Compile with default pattern detection → transitive closure\nrecursive_compiler:compile_recursive(ancestor/2, [target(r)], _Code1),\nformat('--- Pattern: transitive_closure ---~n~w~n', [_Code1]),\n\n%% Write to the R cell\nuser:nb_write('r_ancestor_tc', '.code', _Code1),\nwriteln('\\nR code written to cell: r_ancestor_tc').",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "compile_ancestor_tc",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 10: The Generated R Code (Transitive Closure)\n\nThis cell is overwritten by the compiler with BFS graph traversal code.\nRun the `compile_ancestor_tc` cell above first.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "r",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "# This cell will be overwritten by the compiler.\n# Run 'compile_ancestor_tc' above first.",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "r",
        "name": "r_ancestor_tc",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 11: Query the Generated R Program\n\nThe generated definitions and embedded parent facts persist in the R kernel, so this cell verifies both reachability APIs.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "r",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "descendants <- ancestor_all(\"alice\")\ncat(\"Descendants of alice:\", paste(descendants, collapse = \", \"), \"\\n\")\ncat(\"alice is ancestor of eve:\", ancestor_check(\"alice\", \"eve\"), \"\\n\")\ncat(\"alice is ancestor of frank:\", ancestor_check(\"alice\", \"frank\"), \"\\n\")",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "r",
        "name": "test_r_ancestor",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 12: Inspect the Experimental Bash Fallback\n\nNow we compile the **same predicate** while skipping the transitive-closure pattern. The compiler falls through to its generic tail-recursion Bash scaffold.\n\nThis demonstrates target-selection and skip-option plumbing, but the generated Bash contains an explicit TODO and is **not a behavior-preserving implementation of ancestor/2**. Treat it as compiler-development output, not as an equivalent program.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "%% Compile ancestor/2 again, but skip transitive closure → falls to tail recursion\n%% Also target bash to show cross-language compilation\nrecursive_compiler:compile_recursive(\n    ancestor/2,\n    [target(bash), skip(transitive_closure)],\n    _Code2),\nformat('--- Pattern: tail_recursion (bash) ---~n~w~n', [_Code2]),\n\n%% Write to the bash cell\nuser:nb_write('bash_ancestor_alt', '.code', _Code2),\nwriteln('\\nBash code written to cell: bash_ancestor_alt').",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "compile_ancestor_skip",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "# This cell will be overwritten by the compiler.\n# Run 'compile_ancestor_skip' above first.",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "bash",
        "name": "bash_ancestor_alt",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 13: Compare the Generated Outputs\n\nUse the Notebook VFS to inspect the complete R implementation and the explicitly experimental Bash scaffold side by side:",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "bash",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "echo \"=== R (transitive closure / BFS) ===\"\necho \"Language: $(cat /nb/r_ancestor_tc/.language)\"\necho \"---\"\ncat /nb/r_ancestor_tc/.code\necho \"\"\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Bash (tail recursion) ===\"\necho \"Language: $(cat /nb/bash_ancestor_alt/.language)\"\necho \"---\"\ncat /nb/bash_ancestor_alt/.code",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "bash",
        "name": "compare_outputs",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      }
    ]
  }
